<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aghmat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aghmat is a town at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Before Marrakesh was founded around 1070 it was the chief town of the region and an early capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a>. The Almoravids used it as a place of exile for deposed Andalusi kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-buluggin">Abd Allah ibn Buluggin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> and al-Mutamid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, whose tomb there is still visited.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aghmat.jpg" alt="Aghmat" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahlamine · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghmate_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
